{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And He appeared to him</b> — the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 18:1): to visit the sick. Rabbi Ḥama bar Ḥanina said: it was the third day from his circumcision, and the Holy One, blessed be He, came and inquired after him. \"And behold, three men\" — the angels who came to him in the likeness of three men: one to bring tidings to Sarah, one to heal Abraham, and one to overturn Sodom. And Raphael, who healed Abraham, went from there to save Lot — but this is not two missions, for he was in another place and was afterward commanded in it, or both of them were for salvation. \"And they ate\" — they appeared as though they ate.\n\nAnd in the Book of the Guide for the Perplexed (II:42) it is said that the pericope is general and particular: Scripture said first that HaShem appeared to him in prophetic visions, and how was this vision? — that he lifted his eyes in the vision and behold three men standing over against him. \"And he said: If now I have found favor in your eyes\" — this is a narration of what he said in the prophetic vision to one of them, the greatest among them. And if in the vision there appeared to him only men eating meat, how could it say \"And YHWH appeared to him\"? For HaShem did not appear to him — not in a vision and not in a thought. And thus it is not found in any of the prophecies. And according to his words, Sarah did not knead cakes nor did Abraham prepare a calf, and Sarah did not laugh — rather, everything is a vision. And if so, this dream came with an abundance of detail like false dreams, for what benefit is there in showing him all this?\n\nAnd so he said there (ibid.) concerning \"And a man wrestled with him\" (Genesis 32:25) that everything is a prophetic vision. But I do not know why he was limping upon his thigh when he awoke, and why he said (Genesis 32:31): \"For I have seen Elohim face to face and my soul was delivered.\" For prophets do not fear dying from prophetic visions, and he had already seen a greater and more august vision than this, for he also saw HaShem the august many times in prophetic vision (see Genesis 28:13, 31:3). And according to his opinion, he would need to say the same concerning Lot — for the angels did not come to his house, nor did he bake them matzot and they ate, but everything was a vision. And if he elevates Lot to the level of prophetic vision, how could the men of Sodom, the wicked and sinful, be prophets? For who told them that men had come to his house? And if everything is the visions of Lot's prophecy, then \"And the angels pressed… Arise, take your wife\" and \"Escape for your life\" (Genesis 19:15, 17) and \"Behold, I have accepted your face\" (ibid. 21) and the entire pericope — all a vision, and Lot remains in Sodom. But he would have to suppose that the actions were actually done of themselves, and the statements in each matter were a vision. And these things contradict Scripture; it is forbidden to listen to them, let alone to believe them.\n\nAnd in truth, every place where the seeing of an angel or the speech of an angel is mentioned in Scripture, it is in a vision or in a dream, for physical senses do not perceive angels — but not prophetic visions, for one who perceives an angel or its speech is not a prophet. For the matter is not as the Rav decrees (Guide II:34 and in Mishneh Torah, Hilkhot Yesodei ha-Torah 7:6), that every prophet besides Moses our Master prophesied through an angel. For they already said (Megillah 3a) concerning Daniel: they were superior to him, for they were prophets and he was not a prophet. And so his book was not written among the book of the Prophets because his dealings were with Gabriel, even though he would appear to him and speak with him while awake, as is said in the vision of the second temple: \"And while I was speaking in prayer, the man Gabriel\" (Daniel 9:21). And likewise the vision of the end of redemption (ibid. 10:4) was while awake, as he was walking with his companions by the side of the river. And Hagar the Egyptian is not included among the prophets, and it is also clear that her experience was not a bat kol as the Rav said (Guide, loc. cit.).\n\nAnd Scripture distinguished the prophecy of Moses our Master from the prophecy of the fathers, as is said (Exodus 6:3): \"And I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob as El Shaddai\" — and this is one of the sacred names for the Creator; it is not an epithet for an angel. And our Sages further taught about the distinction between them and said (Leviticus Rabbah 1:14): What is the difference between Moses and all the prophets? The Rabbis say: all the prophets saw through a speculum that does not shine brightly, as it is written (Hosea 12:11): \"And I multiplied visions and by the hand of the prophets I was likened\" — but Moses saw through a brightly shining speculum, as it is written (Numbers 12:8): \"and the likeness of YHWH he beholds\" — as is explained in Vayikra Rabbah (loc. cit.) and other places (Yevamot 49b), and they did not attribute any of their prophecy to an angel anywhere.\n\nAnd do not wonder because it is written (I Kings 13:18): \"I too am a prophet like you, and an angel spoke to me by the word of YHWH, saying\" — for its meaning is: I too am a prophet like you and I know that this is the angel who spoke to me by the word of YHWH, and this is one of the rungs of prophecy, as the man of Elohim said: \"For so He has commanded me by the word of YHWH\" (ibid. 13:9), and said: \"For He spoke to me by the word of YHWH\" (ibid. 13:17). And our Sages already said (Bamidbar Rabbah 20:3) concerning Balaam, who said: \"And now if it is evil in your eyes, let me return to me\" (Numbers 22:34) — I did not go until the Holy One, blessed be He, said to me: \"Arise, go with them\" (ibid. 22:20), and you say I should turn back! So is his faithfulness. Was it not so that He said to Abraham to offer his son, and afterward: \"And an angel of YHWH called to Abraham… and said: Do not send your hand against the youth\" (Genesis 22:12)? It is usual to say a thing and an angel retracts it, etc.\n\nBehold, the Sages are moved to say that the first statement in which HaShem is mentioned is not equal to the second statement in which it says it was through an angel, but rather it is the way of prophets that they are commanded in prophecy and the command is annulled through an angel — for the prophet knows that it is the word of YHWH. And at the beginning of Vayikra Rabbah (1:9) they said: \"And He called to Moses\" — not like Abraham; concerning Abraham it is written: \"And an angel of YHWH called to Abraham a second time from the heavens\" (Genesis 22:15) — the angel calls and the speech speaks. But here the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I am the One who calls and I am the One who speaks. That is to say, Abraham did not attain prophecy until he first prepared himself to perceive an angel, and he would ascend from that rung to the level of the speech of prophecy; but Moses was prepared for prophecy at all times.\n\nBehold, in every place the Sages are moved to inform us that the seeing of an angel is not prophecy, and those who see angels and speak with them are not included among the prophets — as I mentioned concerning Daniel. Rather, it is a vision called \"uncovering of the eyes,\" as in \"And YHWH uncovered the eyes of Balaam and he saw the angel of YHWH\" (Numbers 22:31), and likewise \"And Elisha prayed and said: YHWH, open now his eyes that he may see\" (II Kings 6:17). But in a place where the angels are mentioned by the name \"men,\" as in this pericope and the pericope of Lot, and likewise \"And a man wrestled with him\" (Genesis 32:25), and likewise \"And a man found him\" (Genesis 37:15) according to the opinion of our Sages (Tanhuma, Vayeshev 2) — it is a created glory in the angels; it is called among those who know \"garment.\" It is perceived by the eyes of flesh among the worthy of soul, such as the pious and the sons of the prophets, and I cannot explain it. And the place where you find the seeing of YHWH and the speech of an angel, or the seeing of an angel and the speech of YHWH — as written in the words of Moses at the beginning of his prophecy (Exodus 3:2–4), and in the words of Zechariah (3:1–2) — I will yet reveal in it words of the living Elohim through hints (see Exodus 3:2).\n\nAnd the matter of \"and they ate\" — the Sages said (Bereshit Rabbah 48:14): one by one they departed. And the matter of their departing you will understand from the matter of Noah, if you merit to it.\n\nAnd behold, the explanation of this pericope: after it said that on the very day Abraham was circumcised (Genesis 17:26), it says that HaShem appeared to him while he was ill from his circumcision, sitting and cooling himself at the entrance of his tent because of the heat of the day, which would weaken him. And it mentioned this to inform us that he was not preparing himself for prophecy — he did not fall on his face and was not praying — and even so, this vision came to him." "<b>In the terebinths of Mamre</b> — to indicate the place where he was circumcised. And this revelation of the Shekhinah to him was an elevation and honor for him, as in what occurred in the Tabernacle: \"And they went out and blessed the people, and the glory of YHWH appeared to all the people\" (Leviticus 9:23) — for because of their exertion in the commandment of the Tabernacle, they merited seeing the Shekhinah. And the revelation of the Shekhinah here and there was not to command them a commandment or for any speech whatsoever, but rather a recompense for the commandment already performed, and to make known that Elohim desired their deeds, as is said (Psalms 17:15): \"I, in righteousness, shall behold Your face; I shall be sated, in awakening, with Your likeness.\" And likewise concerning Jacob it says (Genesis 32:2): \"And angels of Elohim met him\" — and there is no speech there, nor did they innovate anything in him, only that he merited the seeing of angels of the Most High and knew that his deeds were accepted. And so for Abraham, the seeing of the Shekhinah was merit and assurance.\n\nAnd so they said (Mekhilta, Shirata 3) concerning those who descended into the sea, who said: \"This is my El and I will glorify Him\" — a maidservant at the sea saw what Ezekiel the prophet did not see; it was a merit for them at the time of the great miracle that they believed in YHWH and in Moses His servant. And sometimes it comes at a time of wrath: \"And all the congregation said to stone them with stones, and the glory of YHWH appeared in the Tent of Meeting to all the children of Israel\" (Numbers 14:10) — and this would be to shield His righteous servants and for their honor.\n\nAnd do not be troubled by the interruption of the pericope, for the matter is connected. And therefore it said \"And He appeared to him\" and did not say \"And YHWH appeared to Abraham\" — but in the pericope He wished to set forth the honor done to him at the time he performed the circumcision, and said that the Shekhinah was revealed upon him and He sent His angels to him to bring tidings to his wife and also to save Lot his brother for his sake. For Abraham had already been informed of a son from the mouth of the Shekhinah, and Sarah from the mouth of the angel who spoke with Abraham, so that Sarah might hear — as it says \"and Sarah was listening.\" And this is their intention when they said (Sotah 14a): to visit the sick — for there was no speech, only honor for him. And they further said (Bereshit Rabbah 48:4): \"An altar of earth you shall make for Me\" (Exodus 20:24) — and if this one, who built an altar for My name, behold I reveal Myself upon him and bless him, Abraham, who circumcised himself for My name, how much more so! And perhaps they intended yet further by this to say that in the vision of the Shekhinah there was healing for the ailment of the circumcision, for so it ought to be, as it is written (Proverbs 16:15): \"In the light of a king's face, there is life.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The phrase אם לא נראה לו appears to be elliptical; translated hyper-literally. Ramban may be alluding to a longer argument about the impossibility of sense-perception of angels that is compressed here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:07.051283423Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}